<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latgale</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Latgale is the easternmost historical region of Latvia, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daugavpils">Daugavpils</a> and Rēzekne. Unlike the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, it remained under Polish–Lithuanian rule after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-altmark">Truce of Altmark</a> of 1629 and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1772, so it stayed largely Catholic. Many of its people speak Latgalian, a written variety of Latvian, and the region joined independent Latvia in 1918–1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latgale.jpg" alt="Latgale" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guntars Mednis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C4%93zeknes_pilsdrupas._Rezekne_castle_ruins_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
